<header class="page-hero"><div class="wrap"><nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/features/">Features</a><span>/</span><span>Battery health</span></nav><span class="section-tag">True battery health</span><h1>See the health number macOS rounds away.</h1><p class="lede">WhatBattery computes health from the battery's raw maximum and design capacities, shows it to one decimal, and puts cycle count and service condition beside it.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="{{ site.download }}">Download free</a><a class="button secondary" href="/docs/install/">Install guide</a></div></div></header>

<section><div class="wrap feature-lead"><div class="feature-copy"><span class="section-tag">No false precision, no hidden rounding</span><h2>A rounded 100% hides the first signs of wear.</h2><p>System Settings presents a whole number, so anything from 99.5% up reads as 100%. Fine at a glance, useless for spotting a trend. WhatBattery works from the underlying mAh figures and keeps one decimal place.</p><p>It also shows cycle count, temperature, voltage, the current charge state, and the same Normal or Service Recommended condition macOS uses. Where macOS's own capacity percentage differs from the unrounded figure, both are shown rather than leaving you to wonder which is right.</p><ul class="benefit-list"><li>Health computed from nominal capacity ÷ design capacity</li><li>Apple's own service condition, not a competing verdict</li><li>macOS's rounded figure shown alongside when the two differ</li><li>Health, cycles and condition are free; the raw mAh capacities are Pro</li></ul></div><img src="/img/screenshot-mac.png" alt="WhatBattery Mac overview showing battery health, capacity, cycle count, temperature, and service condition" width="440" /></div></section>

<section class="soft-section"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><span class="section-tag">The honest boundary</span><h2>It tells you what the battery reports and what cannot be known.</h2><p>There is no dependable public signal that proves a battery is genuine. WhatBattery does not add a green “genuine” badge to incomplete evidence. It shows the service condition, capacities, health, cycles, and identifiers the system can actually support.</p></div><div class="card-grid"><article class="card"><h3>Matches macOS</h3><p>The condition is read from the system, so a service warning agrees with System Settings.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Does not control charging</h3><p>WhatBattery observes. It never enforces a charge limit or writes to the battery.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Keeps missing data missing</h3><p>If the system refuses a reading, the app shows that it is unavailable instead of guessing.</p></article></div></div></section>

